{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "Amken Revelop Plus" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt), vendor's own
  // product name is "Revelop Plus" per tools/json/amken_revelop_plus.json
  // in the earlephilhower/arduino-pico repo.
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // No genuinely input-only pins confirmed on this board (see pfodWeb
  // NOTE below) — nothing beyond the chip-level default is asserted
  // here.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. boards.txt declares no WiFi radio
  // for this board (plain "rp2040" chip bucket; USB VID/PID 0x2770/
  // 0x7305 and 0x2E8A/0x00C0 per tools/json/amken_revelop_plus.json, no
  // cyw43 references).
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040 family audit, button/LED/NeoPixel tagging +
  // dedicated-hardware pin exclusion, 2026-07-13): NO CHANGE — chipGpios
  // deliberately left empty.
  //
  // Board: Amken "Revelop Plus" — an RP2040-based 3D-printer
  // motion-control mainboard from Amken LLC (Amken3D, amken.us /
  // amken3d.com, Springfield VA, USA), same vendor/family as sibling
  // boards amken_bunny and amken_revelop_es (both audited immediately
  // prior this session). Added to earlephilhower/arduino-pico in PR
  // #2283 ("Add Amken boards", merged 2024-07-19, commit
  // 1f71135a2b31350e40370b84c54c3e19f5e7ba55, co-authored by Amken's
  // H.Keni), alongside three siblings: Amken Bunny, Amken Revelop, Amken
  // Revelop eS. Per the PR description/discussion (WebFetched directly
  // this session): these boards were originally designed for Klipper
  // firmware and are being adapted for Marlin via the Arduino-Pico core;
  // the PR simply lists all four board names with no hardware
  // description, datasheet, schematic, or pinout diagram for any of
  // them, including this one.
  //
  // Sources consulted (WebSearch/WebFetch, 2026-07-13):
  //  1. This board's own pins_arduino.h (variants/rp2040/rp2040/
  //     amken_revelop_plus/pins_arduino.h, 80 lines) — confirmed BYTE-
  //     IDENTICAL to sibling amken_revelop's pins_arduino.h via `diff`
  //     (both files match exactly, including the pre-existing pfodWeb
  //     NOTE comments at lines 40 and 75-77). Contains only two
  //     vendor-authored role comments: "// LEDs" above `PIN_LED (24u)`
  //     and "// TMC UART" above `PIN_SERIAL2_TX/RX (4u/5u)`. Unlike
  //     sibling amken_revelop_es (NeoPixel, LIS2D accelerometer,
  //     MAX31865 RTD sensor, CAN transceiver all separately called out
  //     under their own comment headers), this board's Arduino-core
  //     variant declares NO PIN_NEOPIXEL, NO accelerometer/CAN/MAX31865
  //     macro, and NO separate SPI bus in active use (both PIN_SPI0_*
  //     and PIN_SPI1_* are sentineled to (31u), i.e. not pinned out;
  //     only Wire0 SDA=28/SCL=29 is real). GPIO6,7,8-23,25-29 are a flat,
  //     undifferentiated D-alias block with no board-specific role
  //     comment, identical in structure to amken_bunny's own
  //     undifferentiated block. Despite the "Plus" name suggesting an
  //     expanded/richer board relative to plain "Revelop", the
  //     Arduino-pico board-support source shows NO additional dedicated
  //     hardware whatsoever versus amken_revelop — the two variants are
  //     pin-for-pin, byte-for-byte identical in this file. (Any physical
  //     differences the real "Plus" board has vs. plain "Revelop" —
  //     extra connectors, stepper driver sockets, additional power
  //     stages, etc. — are evidently not reflected in the Arduino core's
  //     GPIO-level pin map, e.g. because they reuse the same MCU pinout
  //     or are wired outside this variant's scope.)
  //  2. raw.githubusercontent.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico/master/
  //     tools/json/amken_revelop_plus.json (PlatformIO board
  //     descriptor): confirms vendor "Amken", product name "Revelop
  //     Plus", url "https://www.amken3d.com", USB VID/PID primary
  //     0x2770/0x7305 and secondary 0x2E8A/0x00C0, 32MB max flash, boot2
  //     "boot2_W25Q32JVxQ_4_padded_checksum.S" — no hardware/pin
  //     description beyond generic RP2040 chip facts (133MHz, 256KB RAM,
  //     250mA max USB power) and upload-protocol list. No mention of an
  //     LED, button, NeoPixel, or any dedicated peripheral.
  //  3. amken3d.com (official vendor site, live 2026-07-13): the same
  //     minimal one-page marketing landing page found during the
  //     amken_bunny/amken_revelop_es audits — only "revelop" ("Compact.
  //     Powerful. Affordable. Innovative." / "Amken's original 3D
  //     printer controller board") and "revelop e-S" ("small, but
  //     mighty... robust with all of the must-have features plus extra
  //     bells and whistles") are named as products. "Revelop Plus" /
  //     "Revelop+" is NOT mentioned anywhere on the current homepage
  //     text — same absence pattern already seen for "Bunny", strongly
  //     suggesting this is an earlier/superseded SKU with no
  //     currently-reachable public product page, manual, schematic, or
  //     datasheet.
  //  4. github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico PR #2283 itself
  //     (WebFetched directly): confirms all four Amken boards were added
  //     together, originally Klipper-firmware boards adapted for
  //     Marlin/Arduino-Pico; contains no pin-level hardware detail for
  //     any of the four, including Revelop Plus.
  //  5. WebSearch across multiple query variations ("Amken Revelop Plus
  //     RP2040 board pinout", "amken3d 'revelop plus' 3D printer
  //     controller", etc.): no independent product page, forum thread,
  //     review, schematic, or pinout diagram for this specific board was
  //     found anywhere — same practical documentation ceiling
  //     encountered for amken_bunny and amken_revelop_es.
  //
  // Given GPIO24's onboard LED existence is vendor-confirmed (via the
  // "// LEDs" comment, same as amken_bunny/amken_revelop) but its drive
  // polarity (active-high vs active-low) is NOT documented anywhere
  // found, this audit's explicit rule ("if you cannot find authoritative
  // vendor documentation, report NO CHANGE rather than guess at polarity
  // from the alias name alone") applies — same outcome as amken_bunny.
  // GPIO24 is left at its full chip-default derived capabilities
  // (digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output) — no led_high/led_low tag
  // applied. GPIO4/5 (TMC UART) are already correctly excluded from the
  // Designer's routable pin list by omission (no static-const alias
  // declared for either, matching this file's own pre-existing pfodWeb
  // NOTE) — no chipGpios override needed, nothing broken to fix. No
  // SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK bus-collision bug applies anywhere: SPI0/
  // SPI1 are both sentineled to (31u) (GPIO31 does not physically exist)
  // and correctly left undeclared as static-const aliases; the only real
  // bus pins are Wire0 SDA=GPIO28/SCL=GPIO29, which carry no LED/button
  // role and are not aliased to any other dedicated hardware, so no
  // collision occurs. No button or NeoPixel alias of any kind is
  // declared on this board.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/amken_revelop_plus/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {},

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — matches this
  // board's own pins_arduino.h A0-A3 = GPIO26-29 declarations exactly.
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
